{
  "dom_id": "step-q133430",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that $a$ is an **irrational** number, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $17$ is rational\n\nII. $17a$ is rational\n\nIII. $a\\cdot 17$ is irrational",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that: \n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. The product of a rational number ($17$) and an irrational number ($a$) is irrational.\n\n- Statement III is true. We know from part II that $17a$ is irrational. And since multiplication is commutative, we conclude that $a\\cdot 17$ is irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
